## 18. MODAL, OVERLAY, AND ROUTING RULES

Use overlays only when they make a workflow easier.

Appropriate overlays include:
- Mobile navigation.
- Demo request dialog, if appropriate.
- A larger product preview.
- Small contextual information panels.

Do not make the whole site dependent on modals.

Every modal must:
- Have an accessible dialog name.
- Use role="dialog" and aria-modal="true" where appropriate.
- Reference its title using aria-labelledby.
- Move keyboard focus into the dialog when opened.
- Trap keyboard focus inside the dialog.
- Close with Escape unless the operation genuinely requires otherwise.
- Provide a clearly visible close button.
- Restore focus to the triggering element.
- Lock background scrolling while open.
- Support backdrop dismissal where safe.
- Preserve underlying page state and scroll position.
- Use appropriate mobile full-screen or bottom-sheet behavior.
- Avoid nested modals.

Do not use an ordinary overlay to replace normal navigation to a meaningful product page.

Browser history and deep links:
- Use real, meaningful routes for product detail pages if separate pages are implemented.
- If the hero product selection is represented in the URL, support direct links and browser Back/Forward navigation.
- Keep the active selection synchronized with the URL.
- Avoid creating unnecessary history entries for every tiny animation.
- Restore scroll position correctly when navigating Back.
- Ensure direct navigation and refresh work.

Suggested routes:
- /
- /products/hr
- /products/fuel
- /products/pos
- /about
- /contact
- /privacy
- /terms

If building a single-page site, use valid anchor links and a consistent hash strategy rather than pretending these routes exist.

## 19. ADVANCED MOTION DESIGN

Motion must make the interface feel premium and responsive, not chaotic.

Use a deliberate motion system with:
- Short micro-interactions: approximately 120–200ms.
- UI state transitions: approximately 180–300ms.
- Section entrance transitions: approximately 400–700ms.
- Hero product transitions: approximately 350–650ms.

Use cubic-bezier easing curves with natural acceleration and deceleration.

Recommended motion techniques:
- Text reveal using opacity and modest vertical translation.
- Product preview crossfades and coordinated position changes.
- Slight scale transitions on the hero dashboard.
- Staggered entrance of dashboard cards.
- Subtle background color interpolation between product themes.
- Animated tab indicators.
- Button hover and press feedback.
- Gentle chart drawing.
- Scroll-triggered section reveals.
- Subtle depth movement between dashboard layers.
- Animated SVG line illustrations.
- Small floating notification cards.
- Smooth accordion expansion.
- Carefully controlled parallax on large screens.

Implementation preferences:
- Use Framer Motion / Motion for React if compatible with the project.
- Use CSS transitions for simpler state changes.
- Use Lucide React for consistent interface icons.
- Use CSS transforms and opacity for high-performance animation.
- Use IntersectionObserver for viewport-triggered animation where useful.
- Avoid animating expensive layout properties unnecessarily.
- Clean up observers and event listeners.
- Avoid multiple animation libraries with overlapping responsibilities.

Do not use scroll hijacking.

Do not lock users into long cinematic sequences.

Do not require a user to wait for an animation before interacting.

Do not animate every element continuously.

Honor prefers-reduced-motion. When reduced motion is enabled, remove unnecessary parallax, autoplay, and large movement while preserving functional state changes.

